<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Erskine Murray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1844</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-elphinstone-erskine">James Erskine</a> Murray was a Scottish adventurer who in 1843 sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> hoping, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-brooke">James Brooke</a> in Sarawak, to win a territory of his own. His attempt to gain trading concessions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Kutai on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahakam-river">Mahakam River</a> ended in fighting in early 1844, in which he was killed. The incident prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to tighten their hold on eastern Borneo, and Kutai accepted Dutch overlordship that year.</p></div>
